          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-e261412cd46c\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly avows as true that she now loves the addressee solely for his soul. The utterance defines the object and basis of her love rather than undertaking a commitment. Its exclamatory delivery does not independently indicate reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration has relational consequences and may reassure the addressee, but the text supplies no distinct commitment or undertaking sufficient for P.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Roxane directly addresses her interlocutor in dramatic dialogue and says that she loves him for his soul alone.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The contrast between “toi-même” and “ton âme” presents Roxane's love as having shifted from physical attraction to devotion to the addressee's inner self. In the wider play, this is dramatically ironic: Roxane addresses Christian while the soul and eloquence she has come to love are substantially manifested through Cyrano's words, which she still attributes to Christian. That mistaken attribution affects the declaration's object and dramatic meaning but not its predominantly truth-conditional force as a sincere avowal of her understood emotional state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Et ce n'est plus que pour ton âme que je t'aime !”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense declaration strongly supports T: Roxane avows love and specifies that its basis is the addressee's soul.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“toi-même enfin l'emporte sur toi-même”\",\"supports\":\"The preceding contrast frames the declaration as a considered claim about the addressee's inner self prevailing over outward appearance, not as an unmediated verbal reflex.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The line ends with an exclamation mark and follows “Eh bien !”\",\"supports\":\"These features establish emphatic delivery but, without evidence of involuntary blurting or affective discharge, do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the wider plot, Roxane believes she is responding to Christian's soul and eloquence, although Cyrano has supplied much of the language through which that apparent soul was revealed.\",\"supports\":\"This supplies the declaration's dramatic irony and explains why its sincere avowal can rest on a mistaken attribution without ceasing to be T.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"contribution\":\"Knowledge of Cyrano's authorship of Christian's love language clarifies the declaration's dramatic irony and mistaken attribution while leaving its core force as Roxane's sincere avowal intact.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core utterance as an avowal of love. Its emphasis, relational significance, and dramatic irony are adequately represented as contextual features; no independent P, E, or residual O force is required.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
